<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neoclassicism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neoclassicism was the dominant movement in European art and architecture from the mid-eighteenth to the early nineteenth century, taking the art of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as its model. It was stimulated by the excavations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herculaneum">Herculaneum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> from 1738 and 1748 and by the writings of Johann Joachim Winckelmann, and its leading figures included Jacques-Louis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and Antonio Canova.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neoclassicism.jpg" alt="Neoclassicism" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kimberly Vardeman from Lubbock, TX, USA · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Psyche_revived_by_cupid%27s_kiss,_Paris_2_October_2011_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
